r  distance  stay to the south east of the storm and  enjoy the view  and whether you're a storm chaser out  chasing or simply  caught in your car when a tornado  strikes there are some basic things you  should know  to stay safe as possible if you're in an  area where there is a nearby shelter or  building abandon your car  and get to the shelter immediately but  what if you are  on an open road should you hide under an  overpass  should you get into a ditch  never take shelter under an overpass  while it may look like a sturdy  structure do not get out of your car and  hide  under a bridge hiding in the supports  under the bridge puts you in a very  vulnerable situation  first the winds of the trundle increase  with height by  climbing up into the bridge supports you  are actually exposing yourself to higher  wind speeds  second the winds of the trailer are  forced to squeeze through the  area under the bridge and are therefore  much faster in this location  finally debris is often found lodged  in the area where you would take shelter  where the ground  kind of meets the overpass above people  have been found  crushed to death there by flying debris  now i was taught when i was younger to  abandon a car and seek shelter in a  nearby ditch  this is no longer advised modern cars  have many safety features that are  designed to keep passengers from injury  airbags seat belts safety glass and  crumple zones protect the occupants  stay in your car if you find if you  cannot find  shelter nearby a weak tornado may  produce 110 mile an hour  winds while these winds can push your  car around and possibly roll it over  i would much rather be in the car than  outside and have to deal with these same  winds  if you're outside on the ground your car  might roll over  while you are hanging onto some grass in  the ditch  plus if the tornado is strong enough to  throw your car  it will pull you right out of the ditch  and throw you too  fema is very clear on their website  ready.gov  that the new protocol is to stay in your  vehicle  tornadoes are a part of life for many  around the world  just like with most things we fear  understanding the tornado in its parent  storm the supercell  is half the battle in staying safe and  losing our fears  you can now use the radar technology i  have taught you to know exactly where  the most dangerous parts of these storms  are found you can use the tools we have  discussed to predict  where they are forecast to form and  monitor  the atmosphere conditions that are  conducive to supercell formation  turn your fear into fascination  and use your knowledge to stay safe  [Music]  do  [Music]  [Music]  do  [Music]  do  [Music]  do  [Music]  you

