eam hitting tree branches  shingles and pieces of siding or lumber  that have been thrown hundreds of feet  into the air by the tornadic winds 190  mile per hour winds can level most  structures  and strip the branches and bark from  large  established trees but as i was looking  through  all the pictures of the damage this  particular picture caught my eye  do you see the swirl marks in the fields  that were cut from this tornado  these marks can only be made by a  multi-vortex tornado  small tornadoes those that are at most a  couple of hundred feet wide  have different internal structure than  those that are much wider  small tornadoes don't produce striations  in the fields like this  the internal structure of a small  tornado is such that the winds in the  center of the tornado  ascend rapidly sucking debris upwards  into the storm  as the tornado strengthens it often gets  wider  and undergoes a process called vortex  breakdown  strong centrifugal forces within the  funnel create an opening in the center  of the tornado  where the air pressure drops off  dramatically  within this opening air descends from  above  and creates a very small downdraft in  the center of the tornado  this downdraft widens the tornado a good  analogy for this process is to imagine  a potter creating a clay bowl on a  potter's wheel  the mound of clay is transformed into a  bowl as the potter pushes  his or her thumbs into the center in the  same way  the tornado's downdraft widens it  fundamental principles in physics tell  us that  as the radius of a rotating system  increases  its velocity will decrease this is  exactly what happens in the tornado  as it widens its rotational winds slow  down  so why does the tornado strengthening  why does this happen when the tornado is  getting wider  tornadoes that widen like this undergo a  process called vortex  breakdown on the edge of the tornado air  is rapidly ascending  in the center it is rapidly descending  where the rising air and sinking air  meet small-scale circulations called  suction vortices form a tornado that  goes through vortex breakdown  essentially breaks apart into several  smaller tornadoes  the larger circulation still exists but  now  embedded within its circulation are  several smaller  tornadoes this is when the tornado is at  its  most dangerous one it is very wide  the large-scale winds are still spinning  at triple digit speeds  and now cover a larger area and two  the suction vortices are spinning at  triple digit speeds themselves  have you ever heard of someone talking  about tornado damage where they say  it destroyed this house but left the one  right next to it almost  undamaged we often thought that  tornadoes could hop around  or slither back and forth like a snake  let me assure you that tornadoes do not  hop and they don't slither back and  forth  like the tornado from the wizard of oz  so where did all these  strange stories come from suction  vortices  are often hidden from view they are  masked by the  by all the dust and debris and  condensation in the tornado  so they are rarely seen there is  evidence of suction vortices  like the picture of the field with all  the striations but it was not until  modern  high-resolution doppler radar began  observing them did we understand how  they formed  and how they destroy things each suction  vortex  may only be 50 to 100 feet wide as it  swirls around  inside the larger tornado it may take  out one home entirely but leave the  neighbor's house  standing look at the path of this  tornado from above  do you notice how straight it is in fact  when we look at damaged photos like this  from nearly  all tornadoes we find them to be quite  straight  this is evidence that tornadoes do not  slide back and forth like many believe  but many of you have probably seen a  picture like this  is it fake i just told you that  tornadoes don't behave like the tornado  from the wizard of oz  well this one is not fake it's a picture  of a tornado in its rope stage  like all other parts of the supercell  the tornado has a life cycle  a telltale sign that a tornado is near  the end of its life  is if it takes on this rope shape  will this tornado slide back to the  other side no  it will simply continue to elongate in  this direction until it shears apart and  dissipates  the reason it takes on this shape is  because the rear flank of the storm has  completely  wrapped itself under the updraft and  kicked the base of the tornado out  from under the mesocyclone it will not  recover from this  the supercell however can recover and  begin a new stage of development  as the rear flank undercuts the main  updraft of the old tornado  a new updraft tends to form to the  southeast of the old one  it forms here because this is where the  warm moist unstable air is found  as the new mesocyclone forms the  supercell has the potential to form  another tornado some supercells can go  through  many cycles and produce a family of  tornadoes  each tornado track begins just a few  miles after the old one ends  and it is offset to the southeast by a  few miles  the november 17 2013 supercell in  illinois produced  five different tornadoes the most  famous tornado family event occurred  during the super outbreak of 1974.  this image shows how several supercells  went through many life cycles  and produced numerous tornadoes look  closely  at the tornado tracks that started south  of decatur illinois  one supercell produced nine different  tornadoes as it tracked from central  illinois to northern indiana  see how their tracks are staggered  supercells can often produce seemingly  incredible tornadic events  check out this picture of the twin wedge  tornadoes  from pilger nebraska in 2014.  there is evidence that these tornadoes  formed from the same supercell as it  cycled the first radar dissipated  shortly after this picture was taken  it is a rare event to see two tornadoes  from the same supercell  spinning in the same direction  now tornadoes can form in non-supercell  storms too  just like the water spout and the land  spout forms a  strong like horizontal wind shear when  they do form  land spouts are typically found along  the leading edge of squall lines  the winds ahead of the squall line just  like the supercell  often come from the southwest in the  united states  behind the leading edge of the squall  line the winds shift quickly and are  often from a westerly direction  these winds cross small  vertically aligned circulations can form  between  and basically be stretched into the  updraft of the squall line  the land spouts that form along the  leading edge of the squall line  are often very weak and rarely produce  damage greater than  that of the squall line itself  tornado-like vortices  also form in fire just like the water  spout and land spout  horizontal windshear can create small  eddies  like a whirlwind the heat from the fire  causes the air to rapidly ascend  this stretches the fiery vortex  vertically causing it to spin  faster sometimes these fiery vortices  can move quickly as they're blown by the  wind  they can easily spread forest fires and  they're incredibly  intensely hot  tornado research is difficult to  properly observe a tornado you must get  close to one  one of nature's most intense expressions  of severe weather  early research was pioneered in the work  of dr  theodore fujita but his work almost  never came to be on august 9  1945 the united states planned to drop  its second of  two nuclear bombs on japan the target  city was kokura  but cloud cover forced the bombers to  abandon the target city and hit the  secondary target a city called nagasaki  a 24 year old ted fujita had just  finished his bachelor's of science and  was working on his doctorate studying  typhoons  his life was spared because of cloud  cover  vegeta studied the blast damage of the  nuclear bomb  now when he immigrated to the united  states he pioneered research and  thunderstorm downbursts  and frequently referenced the  similarities between the damage caused  uh by the nuclear bomb and damage  patterns from downbursts  he recognized that it was nearly  impossible to measure the winds  of turn of a tornado directly the winds  and debris it carried would destroy an  anemometer  rather than trying to measure the winds  directly dr fujita set out  to measure the winds of a tornado by  examining the damage they caused  his original scale had 13 levels  f0 to f12  these levels were created to match  nautical wind speeds measured  by the beaufort scale and aeronautical  speeds measured by the mach scale  the beaufort scale measures the force of  the wind and relates it  to sea conditions such as wave height  and appearance  the mach number is simply a ratio of the  actual speed of an object  over the speed of sound on the original  fujita scale  an f1 was the equivalent of the 12th  value on the beaufort scale and had a  had a speed  approximately 74 miles per hour the same  speed is the minimum wind speed  threshold for a category one strength  hurricane  the chart shows the values of the f  scale  although the scale goes to f12 the f5  category corresponds to wind speeds that  range from 261 miles an hour  to 318 miles per hour in fujita's  research he commented on how difficult  it would be for tornadic winds  to exceed this category therefore this  highest category on the operational f  scale  was a 5. the f scale uses damage to  estimate wind speeds  an experienced survey crew is assigned  to every reported tornado  and the damage they survey is  cross-referenced to vegeta's descriptive  scale  each segment of the tornado is tornado's  path i should say is ranked  but the overall ranking that the tornado  receives corresponds to its worst damage  there were a few drawbacks to this  methodology  what if the tornado didn't hit anything  an f5 tornado would do the same amount  of damage to a plowed cornfield  as an f1 what if the tornado hit poorly  hit a poorly constructed home  it may receive a much higher ranking  simply because it was easier to destroy  what if the survey crew was  inexperienced the subjective scale could  be easily misinterpreted  but perhaps the weakest point is how it  is used  a damaged survey must be completed  before the tornado's  rank can be assigned unlike what  hollywood wants you to believe  you cannot see a tornado and immediately  call out its f scale rank  dr fujita's work was pioneering and his  legacy lives on the groundbreaking work  of his students  in 2007 the fujita scale was upgraded to  the new  enhanced vegeta scale the ef scale  upgrade  was implemented to correct some of these  issues its goal was to standardize the  original scale and remove some of its  ambiguity  the ef scale expanded on the original by  adding in  28 separate damage indicators that could  be used by the surveyors  to more accurately estimate wind speeds  so for example  the new scale allowed for the comparison  of damage between hardwood  and a softwood tree it also had new  categories for different styles of  buildings  damage to a building like a walmart  would be different when compared to a  fast food restaurant  or a three-story apartment building or a  strip mall or an automotive showroom  each of these is now included in the new  scale  one of the bigger issues was in the  change  of the wind speed thresholds for each  level of the new ef scale  an f5 tornado produced winds in excess  of 261 miles an hour  in ef5 you'd only have winds of 200  miles an hour  on may 3 1999 the most powerful tornado  in recorded history blast through moore  oklahoma  a mobile doppler radar called the  doppler on wheels  measured winds of 301 miles an hour plus  or minus about 20 miles an hour  this tornado easily achieved an f5  rating as it completely swept homes off  of their foundations  this was the last tornado to be given an  f5 rating  eight years and one day later a powerful  supercell  thunderstorm formed in kansas its  northeast track targeted the town of  greensburg kansas  this small town had a population close  to 1500 people  it was textbook it had a large forward  flank and a well-defined hook echo  it was also after sunset storm spotters  have great difficulty spotting tornadoes  after dark  raider meteorologists at the national  weather service were the only people  who could still see this storm after  dark  warnings went out for the town of  greensburg and the people took  shelter this picture shows what the city  looked like before the tornado hit  this is the same scene after it passed  to my amazement only 11 people lost  their lives that night  the advance warnings guided by doppler  radar saved  thousands of lives damage assessment  crews from the national weather service  noted that 95  of the structures in the town had been  damaged  they ranked the tornado as an ef5 the  first  five ranked tornado on the new scale  although greensburg has since been  rebuilt in a sustainable and  modern way less than half of the  population resides there now  a lot of tornado research has been has  been conducted  on the basis of increasing the tornado  warning time  since 2010 the average warning time is  exceeded  20 minutes and in some cases 30 minutes  some research has suggested that  extending this time beyond 30 minutes  may not be a good thing the concern is  that extending the warning time may  cause it to  lose its immediacy if it became normal  for tornado warnings to have a 30 to 45  minute lead time  people may not decide to take cover  right away  instead they may make the decision that  may put them  in harm's way in the blockbuster movie  twister from 1996  the main characters are a group of storm  chasers their mission  is to place an instrument pack called  dorothy in the path of a tornado  dorothy would then release hundreds of  small sensors into the storm  that would collect and radio back  information about the storm  well the idea was taken from the  research of dr  lou wicker from the university of  illinois in 1982 lou and his colleagues  designed  toto total tornado observatory  lou's research was abandoned though due  to the difficulty of getting such  an instrument in the path of a tornado  plus it did not have small sensors  connected to soda cantilevers  seeing into the tornado is now done with  high  resolution radar tornado research took a  giant leap forward  when dr josh werman designed the first  doppler on wheels  the idea was to strap a raider onto the  back of a truck and get it as close to  the storm as possible and  scan it storm chasing has been a hobby  for  many people for decades but the  invention of the mobile doppler radar  took storm chasing to a new  level numerous federally funded field  campaigns  like vortex vortex 2 and vortex  southeast  have used doppler on wheels to safely  scan tornadoes from a nearby location  in fact these raiders have participated  in over 20  field studies since they were first  designed  the data collected by these radars has  revolutionized our understanding of  tornadoes  and lengthened warning times these  observations have helped us better model  these storms  and forecast them however at the same  time that tornado research has blossomed  so is the prevalence of amateur storm  chasing  let me mention some key precautions to  keep yourself safe  should you or anyone you know decide to  try storm chasing  always have a point of contact someone  who knows where you are  know your road network never chase on  dirt roads or roads that go  into forested areas you can get stuck or  find yourself in a position  where you can't see the tornado always  bring a cell phone that has access to  radar imagery  there are great radar apps that you can  use that will show you  your position with respect to the  storm's position  always have an out know exactly where  you will go if things get too intense  never try to be a hero keep your  distance  stay to the south east of the storm and  enjoy the view  and whether you're a storm chaser out  chasing or simply  caught in your car when a tornado  strikes there are some basic things you  should know  to stay safe as possible if you're in an  area where there is a nearby shelter or  building abandon your car  and get to the shelter immediately but  what if you are  on an open road should you hide under an  overpass  should you get into a ditch  never take shelter under an overpass  while it may look like a sturdy  structure do not get out of your car and  hide  under a bridge hiding in the supports  under the bridge puts you in a very  vulnerable situation  first the winds of the trundle increase  with height by  climbing up into the bridge supports you  are actually exposing yourself to higher  wind speeds  second the winds of the trailer are  forced to squeeze through the  area under the bridge and are therefore  much faster in this location  finally debris is often found lodged  in the area where you would take shelter  where the ground  kind of meets the overpass above people  have been found  crushed to death there by flying debris  now i was taught when i was younger to  abandon a car and seek shelter in a  nearby ditch  this is no longer advised modern cars  have many safety features that are  designed to keep passengers from injury  airbags seat belts safety glass and  crumple zones protect the occupants  stay in your car if you find if you  cannot find  shelter nearby a weak tornado may  produce 110 mile an hour  winds while these winds can push your  car around and possibly roll it over  i would much rather be in the car than  outside and have to deal with these same  winds  if you're outside on the ground your car  might roll over  while you are hanging onto some grass in  the ditch  plus if the tornado is strong enough to  throw your car  it will pull you right out of the ditch  and throw you too  fema is very clear on their website  ready.gov  that the new protocol is to stay in your  vehicle  tornadoes are a part of life for many  around the world  just like with most things we fear  understanding the tornado in its parent  storm the supercell  is half the battle in staying safe and  losing our fears  you can now use the radar technology i  have taught you to know exactly where  the most dangerous parts of these storms  are found you can use the tools we have  discussed to predict  where they are forecast to form and  monitor  the atmosphere conditions that are  conducive to supercell formation  turn your fear into fascination  and use your knowledge to stay safe  [Music]  do  [Music]  [Music]  do  [Music]  do  [Music]  do  [Music]  you
 

